WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.147
Alder had 13 readmissions. The reference
clinic, given the same patients, expects 8.

00:00:06.187 --> 00:00:07.777
Is that a real difference?

00:00:07.817 --> 00:00:12.617
Or could a clinic exactly like the
reference have had 13, just by luck?

00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:15.564
To answer, we need to
see what luck looks like.

00:00:15.604 --> 00:00:19.080
We'll start with Dune, because
Dune has only 12 patients,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.629
and we can watch every one of them.

00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:25.258
Dune had 7 readmissions against 4.8 expected:

00:00:25.298 --> 00:00:30.906
the highest raw rate in the cast.
So make a guess. Is Dune unusual?

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.721
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:34.761 --> 00:00:36.839
Today: unequal coins.

00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:43.714
Suppose Dune were exactly like the
reference. Then each patient is a coin.

00:00:43.754 --> 00:00:49.188
For the six lower-risk patients, it
comes up readmitted 18% of the time.

00:00:49.228 --> 00:00:52.467
For the six higher-risk patients, 62%.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.760
Flip all twelve and count. Three readmissions.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.689
Flip again: five. And again: three.

00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:03.316
Do it two thousand times, and
the counts pile up around 4.8.

00:01:03.356 --> 00:01:08.484
That pile is what luck looks like, for a
clinic like the reference with Dune's patients.

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:12.434
We don't actually have to flip anything.

00:01:12.474 --> 00:01:16.134
The pile can be worked out
exactly, one coin at a time.

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:20.767
Each new coin splits every bar in
two. One part stays where it is:

00:01:20.807 --> 00:01:24.873
no readmission. The other
moves one step to the right.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.526
After twelve coins, the
chance of exactly 5 is 26%.

00:01:32.566 --> 00:01:37.830
Of 6, 19%. Of 7, 9%. And of 8 or more, 4%.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.575
Dune's count was 7. For a
clinic just like the reference,

00:01:44.615 --> 00:01:47.257
the chance of 7 or more is 13%.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.500
Two refinements. Counts move in whole steps,

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:55.531
so we take only half of the bar
at 7 itself. That brings it to 8%.

00:01:55.571 --> 00:02:00.742
And a clinic can surprise us in either
direction, so we double it: 16%.

00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:06.977
That is the p-value. About one time in six,
luck alone does this. Dune is not flagged.

00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:16.655
Now Alder. Thirty coins: 24 at 18%,
and 6 at 62%. The pile centres on 8.

00:02:16.695 --> 00:02:19.562
Alder's 13 sits far out in the tail.

00:02:19.602 --> 00:02:26.090
The chance of 13 or more is two and a
half percent, and the p-value is 3.5%.

00:02:26.233 --> 00:02:29.771
That is below the usual
5% line. Alder is flagged:

00:02:29.811 --> 00:02:33.666
more readmissions than
luck comfortably explains.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.988
So the clinic with the highest raw
rate is not flagged, and Alder is.

00:02:38.028 --> 00:02:42.299
Twelve patients leave a lot of
room for luck. Thirty leave less.

00:02:42.433 --> 00:02:46.188
For the others: Birch, 45%. Cedar, 99%.

00:02:46.228 --> 00:02:51.636
And Elm, 11%, which is
simply its whole bar at zero.

00:02:53.233 --> 00:02:55.830
Why insist that the coins are unequal?

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:00.937
Swap Alder's thirty for thirty
identical coins with the same average.

00:03:00.977 --> 00:03:09.946
The pile gets wider. The p-value rises from
3.5% to 5.2%, and Alder slips under the bar.

00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:16.028
Mixed coins vary less than identical ones.
Use the right pile, and the test is sharper.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.248
So far we built the pile at the reference.

00:03:20.288 --> 00:03:25.886
But we can build it at any gamma, and ask
where Alder's 13 stops being surprising.

00:03:26.033 --> 00:03:30.068
Slide the gamma up, and the pile slides right.

00:03:30.108 --> 00:03:35.614
Between minus 1.46 and plus
0.13, thirteen is unremarkable.

00:03:35.654 --> 00:03:41.352
That is Alder's exact 95% interval.
The reference, at minus 1.52,

00:03:41.392 --> 00:03:46.244
lies just outside it: the
same verdict as the flag.

00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:51.410
Carried through the rising
curve from Episode 11,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:55.663
Alder's ratio lies between 1.04 and 2.26.

00:03:55.703 --> 00:04:01.032
Dune's, between 0.82 and
2.04, which includes 1.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.554
Does the test keep its promise?

00:04:04.594 --> 00:04:09.250
We built fifty crowds in which every
provider truly equals the reference,

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.371
and tested all ten thousand.

00:04:11.411 --> 00:04:16.134
It flagged 4.8%, against the 5% it promises.

00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:20.600
Two cautions. The test treats the
dial and the reference as known.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.219
And a p-value is not the chance
that the clinic is ordinary.

00:04:24.259 --> 00:04:27.625
It is the chance of a count
like this, if it were.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.696
Let's gather it up.

00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:32.591
If a clinic were like the reference,

00:04:32.631 --> 00:04:35.859
each patient would be a coin
with a chance of their own.

00:04:35.899 --> 00:04:39.238
The count's whole distribution
can be worked out exactly,

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:42.877
and the tail beyond what was
observed gives the p-value.

00:04:42.917 --> 00:04:46.211
Turned inside out, the same
test gives an interval.

00:04:46.333 --> 00:04:48.695
Exact is satisfying, but it takes work.

00:04:48.735 --> 00:04:52.032
Are there quicker ways to
nearly the same answer?

00:04:52.072 --> 00:04:54.342
Next time: three shortcuts.

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:58.572
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
